  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another chilly night ahead with Frost \nAdvisories in effect for portions of northern Virginia and \ncentral/northern Maryland. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to meander over the area this evening \nbefore drifting southwest Sunday into Monday. As a result, expect a \ncontinuation of frost and freeze conditions during the overnight \nperiods with seasonable temperatures during the daylight hours. \n\nTonight appears to be the coldest night of the next few given high \npressure directly overhead. Hi-res CAM guidance continues to show \npersistence in regards to widespread frost and freeze conditions \ntonight, especially in locations west of I-95. Have expanded the\nFrost Advisory to include most of the rest of northern VA and to\nI-95 in MD, and inserted a Freeze Warning for northern MD down\ninto portions of northern VA (Fauquier/Culpeper/W Loudoun).\n\nThese locations are also still in the growing season for 2026 while \nlocations west of the Blue Ridge are no longer active. Locations \neast of the advisory (along and east of I-95) will also have a \nchance at some frost in the colder pockets, but should remain \nslightly warmer given the Chesapeake Bay influence. Confidence \nremains lower for an advisory expansion into central \nVirginia/southern MD due to higher dew points and potential cloud \ncover, as a remnant stratocumulus field continues to drift westward \nand possibly banking along the Blue Ridge overnight into early \nSunday morning.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually drift southwest toward the central \nAppalachians Sunday night into Monday morning. Low level flow will \nturn westerly allowing for less of a marine influence Sunday into \nSunday night. This should set us up for one more good radiational \ncooling night (Sunday night) before temperatures warm under return \nflow early in the workweek. Sunday night will be more of a ridge-\nvalley split type of night with cooler conditions in the \nvalleys/cold pockets/locations along and east of I-95 compared to \nthe ridge tops given the sharp surface inversion/decoupling. \n\nOnly minor changes have been made to the previous min temperature \nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10th percentile and \nCanadian Regional).\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower 70s) by \nMonday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the surface. \nMinimal cloud cover and light winds will remain persistent.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry conditions\ncontinue through much of next week. \n\nDry weather continues into the midweek period as surface high \npressure lingers to the south Tuesday and Wednesday. A backdoor dry \nfrontal boundary associated with a low over the Northern Plains \nlikely traverses through the region during this time, but no \nprecipitation is anticipated from this as moisture/PWATs remain low. \nAt most, cloud cover begins to increase during this period, with \ndaytime temperatures approaching the mid-70s as high pressure \npersists.  \n\nLooking ahead, a weak disturbance over the Ohio River Valley \npotentially moves through the Mid-Atlantic late in the week. Impacts \nfrom this look minimal at this time, as PWATs continue to range on \nthe dry side while winds remain light. Overall, no precipitation is \ncurrently anticipated for the entire 7-day forecast period at this \ntime. Dry conditions continue to prevail with seasonable \ntemperatures ranging in the 60s-70s during the day, with overnight \nlows mostly in the 40s throughout the week.",
